Looking at the diff: the only change is in the parse regex, where `seconds?` becomes `seconds` — dropping the optional trailing `s`. Every other unit alternative is untouched.

The `switch` in `parse` still handles `case 'second':`, but with the new regex the string "second" can no longer match as a unit: the alternative `seconds` requires the trailing "s", `secs?` only matches "sec"/"secs", and `s` alone can't satisfy the `$` anchor. So `"1 second"` no longer matches at all (the unit group is optional, but `$` then can't follow the space), and `parse` returns `undefined`.

Observable difference: `ms('1 second')` returned `1000` in ms@2.1.3, but now returns `undefined`. Plurals like `'2 seconds'` still work, and no other unit's singular form was affected, which is why the existing suite (which never parses singular "second" as input) stays green.

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "proof-test.js", "content": "var assert = require('assert');
var ms = require('./');

describe('proof', function () {
  it('should still parse the singular unit "second" as in ms@2.1.3', function () {
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1 second'), 1000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1 SECOND'), 1000);
    assert.strictEqual(ms('1.5 second'), 1500);
    // sanity: plurals and other units are unaffected
    assert.strictEqual(ms('2 seconds'), 2000);
  });
});
"}, "note": "Dropping the '?' in 'seconds?' makes the singular unit 'second' fail the anchored regex, so ms('1 second') now returns undefined instead of 1000. The switch's 'second' case is now unreachable, and since the existing tests never parse singular 'second', the suite stays green."}
```

